  "theory": {
    "intro": "The **Fundamental Theorem of Calculus** links area and primitives. If $F$ is any primitive of a continuous function $f$, then $\\int_{a}^{b} f(x)\\,dx = F(b) - F(a)$. Differentiating an area function undoes integrating, so the two operations are inverses.",
    "facts": [
      "A **primitive** $F$ of $f$ is a function with $F'(x) = f(x)$.",
      "The **area function** $A(x) = \\int_{a}^{x} f(t)\\,dt$ measures signed area from the fixed left end $a$ to the moving right end $x$.\n[tikz]\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\\large}]\n\\draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (6.4,0) node[right] {$t$};\n\\draw[->] (0,-0.5) -- (0,4.8) node[above] {$y$};\n\\fill[gray!35, domain=1.2:4, variable=\\x] (1.2,0) -- plot ({\\x},{1 + 0.12*(\\x-0.6)*(\\x-0.6)}) -- (4,0) -- cycle;\n\\draw[thick, <->, domain=0.2:5.8, smooth, variable=\\x] plot ({\\x},{1 + 0.12*(\\x-0.6)*(\\x-0.6)});\n\\draw[dashed] (1.2,0) -- (1.2,1.04);\n\\draw[dashed] (4,0) -- (4,2.39);\n\\node[below] at (1.2,0) {$a$};\n\\node[below] at (4,0) {$x$};\n\\node at (2.6,0.7) {$A(x)$};\n\\node at (3.6,4.1) {$y=f(t)$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]",
      "The first form of the theorem says $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\int_{a}^{x} f(t)\\,dt = f(x)$, so the area function is a primitive of $f$.",
      "The second form says $\\int_{a}^{b} f(x)\\,dx = F(b) - F(a)$ for any primitive $F$ of $f$.",
      "This is set out as $\\int_{a}^{b} f(x)\\,dx = \\left[F(x)\\right]_{a}^{b}$, meaning substitute $b$ and then subtract the value at $a$.",
      "The constant $+C$ cancels in that subtraction, so a definite integral never carries $+C$.",
      "A definite integral is a number, while an indefinite integral is a function.",
      "Signed area counts any part of the region below the $x$-axis as negative."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Find a primitive $F(x)$ of the integrand and write it in square brackets with the limits.",
      "Substitute the upper limit $b$ into $F$.",
      "Subtract the value of $F$ at the lower limit $a$.",
      "Simplify to a single number."
    ]
  },
